                <pb facs="0123" n="11" sameAs="frus:frus1935v03/pg11"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.01 Manchuria/1213: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-01-19T12:00:00+09:00">January 19,
                        1935—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received January 19—5:06 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>10. The Military Attaché reports that, according to information which he obtained
                    confidentially from the Japanese Army, a small detachment from the Kwantung army
                    is operating near Tatan, Jehol, about 130 miles south of Taerhhao (Chahar)
                    against bandit troops. These bandits are said to be the remains of Chang
                    Hsueh-liang’s troops which did not retreat south of the Wall but took up their
                    residence in Western Jehol and are now a nuisance and menace to peaceful
                    population in that region. The Japanese intend to drive them out of Jehol. The
                    military authorities characterize the movement as being of no importance, and as
                    having no other purpose than that stated.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
